===Etymology===
{{multiple images
|direction = vertical
|image1 = Le bacche del vischio.jpg
|caption1 = A [[European mistletoe]] ({{taxlink|Viscum album|species|noshow=1}}; ''sense 1'') plant
|image2 = Woolson Spice Co. (3092983603).jpg
|caption2 = A [[Victorian]] [[trade card]] depicting mistletoe hung up as a [[Christmas]] [[decoration]] ''(sense 2)'', with two people [[kiss#Verb|kissing]] beneath it
}}

From {{inh|en|enm|mistel-tō}}, {{m|enm|mistelto||European mistletoe; hardened berries of mistletoe used as beads}}, from {{inh|en|ang|misteltān}}, {{m|ang|mistiltān}}, {{m|ang|mistil-tān||mistletoe}}, from {{m|ang|mistel||mistletoe; basil; birdlime}} + {{m|ang|tān||sprig, twig}} (which was mistaken for the plural of {{m|ang|tā||toe}}),<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=mistel-tō, ''n.''|id=MED28273|accessdate=19 July 2018}}</ref> equivalent to {{compound|en|mistle|tan|t2=twig}}. ''Mistel'' is derived from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*mihstilaz||mistletoe; basil}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₃meyǵʰ-||to urinate}}. The English word is cognate with {{cog|da|mistelten}}, {{cog|is|mistilteinn}}.

